seed() in Python
# Think of it like planting a seed to grow random numbers.
# The number you give to seed() will control the random results.

🔸 Why use it?
# Because random() is normally unpredictable.But if you want the same random numbers every time (for testing, for example), you use seed().

🧪 Two ways to use it:
✅ 1. seed()                     — no number
This uses the current time as the seed. So results will always change.
✅ 2. seed(0)                   — with a number
This makes random results repeat every time you run the code.

🔁 Example:
from random import random, seed
seed(0)  # Fix the starting point
for i in range(5):
    print(random())
This will always print the same 5 numbers:
#
0.844421851525
0.75795440294
0.420571580831
0.258916750293
0.511274721369
