import pygame

run = True
width = 400
height = 100
pygame.init()
screen = pygame.display.set_mode((width, height))
font = pygame.font.SysFont(None, 48)
text = font.render("Welcome to pygame", True, (255, 255, 255))
screen.blit(text, ((width - text.get_width()) // 2, (height - text.get_height()) // 2))
pygame.display.flip()
while run:
    for event in pygame.event.get():
        if event.type == pygame.QUIT\
        or event.type == pygame.MOUSEBUTTONUP\
        or event.type == pygame.KEYUP:
            run = False



#########
🧪 What this code does:
This tiny program shows a small white window with the words:
"Welcome to pygame"

It stays open until you:
❌ close the window
🖱️ click the mouse inside it
⌨️ press any key

🐢 Line-by-line Explanation (Very Simple)

import pygame
👉 Tell Python: "I want to use the pygame package."


run = True
👉 This means: "Keep the window open as long as run is True."


width = 400
height = 100
👉 We're setting the window size: 400 pixels wide, 100 pixels tall.


pygame.init()
👉 This tells pygame: "Get everything ready before we use you."


screen = pygame.display.set_mode((width, height))
👉 This opens a new window that is 400x100 in size, and stores it in screen.


font = pygame.font.SysFont(None, 48)
👉 This chooses a font style (default) and font size 48.


text = font.render("Welcome to pygame", True, (255, 255, 255))
👉 This turns your text into an image:

"Welcome to pygame" = the message

True = make it smooth and clear (anti-aliasing)

(255, 255, 255) = color = white


screen.blit(text, ((width - text.get_width()) // 2, (height - text.get_height()) // 2))
👉 This places the text in the center of the screen (horizontally and vertically).

pygame.display.flip()
👉 Show the screen with the message. (It was invisible until now.)


while run:
👉 Start a loop — we’ll keep looping (keep the window open) while run is True.


    for event in pygame.event.get():
👉 Get a list of all things the user does, like clicks or key presses.


        if event.type == pygame.QUIT\
        or event.type == pygame.MOUSEBUTTONUP\
        or event.type == pygame.KEYUP:
            run = False
👉 If the user closes the window, clicks, or presses a key →
we set run = False, and the loop stops → window closes.

✅ Summary (in simple words):
Part	What it does
pygame.init()	Get pygame ready
set_mode(...)	Open the window
font.render(...)	Turn text into something you can show
screen.blit(...)	Put the text on the screen (but still invisible)
display.flip()	Make the screen show up
while run	Keep running until the user quits, clicks, or presses a key

